---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="Sol Fabricator — run a production backend without a platform team" description="Run a production backend without a platform team. Sol Fabricator turns your backend code into a production system — infrastructure, deployment, observability, and operations included. Open source, Apache-2.0, pre-alpha.">
  <main>
    <section class="hero shell">
      <nav class="nav" aria-label="Main navigation">
        <a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="Sol home">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 42 42" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="21" cy="17" r="8"/><path d="M8 29c4-5 8-7 13-7s9 2 13 7"/><path d="M12 34h18"/></svg>
          <span>sol</span>
        </a>
        <div class="nav-links">
          <a href="/how-it-works/">Walkthrough</a>
          <a href="#architecture">Architecture</a>
          <a href="#cloud">Own your cloud</a>
          <a href="#proof">Proof</a>
        </div>
        <a class="nav-cta" href="https://github.com/loganbnielsen/sol">GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </nav>

      <div class="hero-main">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">Open-source software factory</p>
          <h1>Run a production backend<br/><em>without a platform team.</em></h1>
          <p class="lede">Sol turns your backend code into a production system — builds, infrastructure, deployment, observability, and operations included. Run it locally on the same stack as production, then deploy the same contract to Kubernetes in your own cloud.</p>
          <div class="actions">
            <a class="button primary" href="#install">Get started <span>→</span></a>
            <a class="button ghost" href="/how-it-works/">Technical walkthrough</a>
          </div>
          <p class="status-strip"><span class="status-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>Pre-alpha · v0.1.0-alpha · not production-stable<a href="https://github.com/loganbnielsen/sol/blob/main/docs/planning/ROADMAP.md">Roadmap ↗</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="hero-features" aria-label="What Sol gives you">
        <article><b>⌘</b><div><h2>Open source</h2><p>Apache-2.0. Your Kubernetes cluster, your cloud account, your data.</p></div></article>
        <article><b>✦</b><div><h2>OCaml or TypeScript</h2><p>Two first-class application languages behind one contract.</p></div></article>
        <article><b>⚡</b><div><h2>Evidence over magic</h2><p>Qualification runs published from real AWS and GCP.</p></div></article>
      </div>

      <div class="hero-terminal" id="install">
        <div class="hero-terminal-tabs"><span class="active">Terminal</span><span>Deploy</span><span>Status</span><span>Logs</span><span>Rollback</span><button class="copy" aria-label="Copy quickstart commands" data-copy={"sol local infra up\nsol new workspace myapp\ncd myapp && sol up"}>Copy</button></div>
        <div class="hero-terminal-body">
          <pre><span class="prompt">$</span> sol local infra up
<span class="prompt">$</span> sol new workspace myapp
<span class="prompt">$</span> cd myapp && sol up

<span class="success">✓ k3d cluster: redpanda · postgres · loki · grafana</span>
<span class="success">✓ charge_svc deployed · worker consuming events</span>
<span class="success">✓ curl localhost:8080/health → ok</span></pre>
          <aside class="terminal-aside" aria-label="What the local cluster includes">
            <p class="terminal-aside-title">Local cluster</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Redpanda — Kafka and schema registry</li>
              <li>PostgreSQL</li>
              <li>Loki and Grafana</li>
              <li>Prometheus and Pushgateway</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="terminal-aside-note">Same charts as production.</p>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="panel shell" id="how">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="section-label">Local → production</p>
        <h2>“It worked locally”<br/><em>should mean something.</em></h2>
        <p>Sol runs the same stack locally that you ship to production — same Helm charts, same service DNS, same security model. The only difference is scale. One application contract moves from your machine to your cloud.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="spine-flow" aria-label="The path from local to production">
        <article>
          <span>01</span>
          <code>sol local run</code>
          <b>Fast inner loop</b>
          <small>Native processes · one <code>dune build</code> · no image rebuild</small>
        </article>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <article>
          <span>02</span>
          <code>sol up</code>
          <b>Production-shaped</b>
          <small>Real containers · real manifests · a real cluster</small>
        </article>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <article>
          <span>03</span>
          <code>sol deploy prod/aws/us-east-1</code>
          <b>Your real cloud</b>
          <small>AWS · GCP · or any Kubernetes</small>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="spine-contract">Same application contract · one deployment plan</p>
    </section>

    <section class="panel shell" id="architecture">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="section-label">How it works</p>
        <h2>Infrastructure is<br/><em>a build artifact.</em></h2>
        <p>Sol reads your workspace and compiles it. Deployment, wiring, and observability are derived from the code — not hand-authored per environment, and not able to drift between local and production.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-grid">
        <div class="split-panel">
          <p class="split-title">You write</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Routes and handlers, with auth declared per route</li>
            <li>Typed domain events</li>
            <li>SQL migrations</li>
            <li>A Dockerfile per unit</li>
            <li>Service config and secrets in <code>sol.toml</code></li>
            <li>Domain boundaries — one team owns each domain</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="split-panel accent">
          <p class="split-title">Sol derives</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Kubernetes namespaces, Deployments, Services, CronJobs</li>
            <li>Kafka topics, schema registration, consumer groups</li>
            <li>The migration plan and tracking table</li>
            <li>Service identity, metric and log labels, trace ownership</li>
            <li>Image names and release identity</li>
            <li>Observability wiring</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="split-note">Workspace convention — <code>app/&lt;domain&gt;/&lt;name&gt;_&#123;svc,worker,fn&#125;/</code> with a Dockerfile</p>
    </section>

    <section class="deploy-model shell" id="model" aria-labelledby="deploy-model-title">
      <div class="deploy-model-head">
        <div><p class="section-label">The application model</p><h2 id="deploy-model-title">You bring the code.<br/><span>Sol runs the system.</span></h2></div>
        <p>Three primitives cover backend work. Sol discovers them, verifies the runtime contract, builds an immutable artifact, and operates it — locally or in your own cloud.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="primitive-row" aria-label="Sol application primitives">
        <article><code>_svc</code><div><h3>Service</h3><p>Long-running HTTP APIs with per-route auth.</p></div></article>
        <article><code>_worker</code><div><h3>Worker</h3><p>Kafka consumers with retry and dead-letter routing.</p></div></article>
        <article><code>_fn</code><div><h3>Function</h3><p>Scheduled jobs that run once and exit.</p></div></article>
      </div>
      <div class="split-grid">
        <div class="split-panel">
          <p class="split-title">Runtime contract</p>
          <ul>
            <li><code>PORT</code> — the port the container listens on</li>
            <li><code>GET /healthz</code> — liveness and readiness</li>
            <li><code>GET /metrics</code> — Prometheus exposition</li>
            <li><code>SIGTERM</code> — drain and shut down cleanly</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="split-panel">
          <p class="split-title">Application contract</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Auth declared explicitly on every route</li>
            <li>Typed, schema-registry-backed events between domains</li>
            <li>PostgreSQL with typed access and migrations</li>
            <li>Events are the only cross-domain contract — no shared code</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="platform-panel shell" id="cloud">
      <div class="platform-intro">
        <p class="section-label">Own your cloud</p>
        <h2>Runs on Kubernetes.<br/><em>In your account.</em></h2>
        <p class="platform-lede">Sol targets plain Kubernetes, not a proprietary runtime. Deploy straight to your cluster or emit manifests for GitOps — your cloud account, your registry, your data.</p>
        <div class="cloud-choice"><span>Targets</span><b>AWS</b><i>or</i><b>Google Cloud</b><i>or</i><b>any Kubernetes</b><small>AWS and GCP provisioning are experimental; any conformant cluster works.</small></div>
        <div class="platform-benefits">
          <article><span>⬡</span><div><h3>Cloud-agnostic</h3><p>One application model compiles to Kubernetes, wherever it runs.</p></div></article>
          <article><span>◇</span><div><h3>Direct or GitOps</h3><p>Apply to your cluster, or emit manifests for Argo CD to reconcile.</p></div></article>
          <article><span>♢</span><div><h3>Escape hatches</h3><p>Per-service overrides in <code>sol.toml</code>; generated Terraform and manifests are yours to take.</p></div></article>
          <article><span>⌘</span><div><h3>No lock-in</h3><p>Open-source substrate — Kubernetes, Strimzi, Argo CD, Prometheus, Loki, Grafana, Terraform.</p></div></article>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="platform-stack" aria-label="Ownership layers">
        <div class="stack-spine"></div>
        <article class="stack-card stack-app"><span>⌁</span><b>Your App</b></article>
        <article class="stack-card stack-sol"><span>◉</span><b>Sol Factory</b></article>
        <article class="stack-card stack-infra"><span>⬡</span><b>Kubernetes</b></article>
        <article class="stack-card stack-world"><span>⌁</span><b>Your Cloud Account</b></article>
        <p class="stack-note">From code<br/>to production.<span>↖</span></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="panel shell" id="ops">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="section-label">Observability &amp; operations</p>
        <h2>Wired in from the first message.<br/><em>Operated with evidence.</em></h2>
        <p>Logs, metrics, and traces are part of the runtime contract, not a stack you assemble. The same CLI then tells you what is running, what changed, and how to put it back.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-grid">
        <div class="split-panel">
          <p class="split-title">Built in, not bolted on</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Structured logs shipped to Loki</li>
            <li>Prometheus metrics exposed per primitive</li>
            <li>W3C trace context across services and Kafka</li>
            <li>Grafana dashboards over one workspace, not per service</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="split-panel accent">
          <p class="split-title">Operated as one workflow</p>
          <ul>
            <li><code>sol status</code> — rollout state and Kubernetes diagnosis</li>
            <li><code>sol releases</code> and <code>sol rollback</code> — inspect and reverse</li>
            <li><code>.sol/runs/</code> — deploy logs that outlive the terminal</li>
            <li>Canary and blue-green rollout from <code>sol.toml</code></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="panel shell" id="proof">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="section-label">Proof</p>
        <h2>Show the work.<br/><em>Publish the evidence.</em></h2>
        <p>Sol is pre-alpha, and we would rather show the receipts than round up. The qualification runs, the end-to-end dogfood, and the current limits are all in the repository.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="proof-grid">
        <article class="proof-card">
          <span class="proof-tag">Qualification</span>
          <h3>Run against real cloud</h3>
          <p>Dated, reproducible qualification runs against real AWS and GCP — the same single-region path we intend to support.</p>
          <a href="https://github.com/loganbnielsen/sol/tree/main/docs/qualification">Read the run records ↗</a>
        </article>
        <article class="proof-card">
          <span class="proof-tag">Dogfood</span>
          <h3>End-to-end, every release</h3>
          <p>New workspace to local cluster to deploy, observe, and roll back — exercised as the release path, not a demo.</p>
          <a href="https://github.com/loganbnielsen/sol/blob/main/docs/guides/TUTORIAL.md">Follow the tutorial ↗</a>
        </article>
        <article class="proof-card">
          <span class="proof-tag">Status</span>
          <h3>Honest about the edges</h3>
          <p>Single-region Kubernetes today. Multi-region, hosted, and cloud provisioning are experimental or not yet built.</p>
          <a href="https://github.com/loganbnielsen/sol/blob/main/docs/planning/ROADMAP.md">See the roadmap ↗</a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="closing shell">
      <div class="closing-sun"></div>
      <p class="section-label">Start with one service</p>
      <h2>Make production<br/><em>boring again.</em></h2>
      <p>Start on k3d locally. Ship to your own Kubernetes cluster when you're ready.</p>
      <div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="https://github.com/loganbnielsen/sol">Star on GitHub <span>→</span></a><a class="button ghost" href="https://github.com/loganbnielsen/sol/blob/main/docs/guides/TUTORIAL.md">Read the tutorial</a></div>
    </section>

    <footer class="shell">
      <div class="footer-brand">
        <a class="brand" href="/"><svg viewBox="0 0 42 42" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="21" cy="17" r="8"/><path d="M8 29c4-5 8-7 13-7s9 2 13 7"/><path d="M12 34h18"/></svg><span>sol</span></a>
        <p>Sol Fabricator — open-source software factory for backend services. Apache-2.0.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="footer-right">
        <nav class="footer-links" aria-label="Project links">
          <a href="/how-it-works/">Walkthrough</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/loganbnielsen/sol">GitHub</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/loganbnielsen/sol/blob/main/docs/guides/TUTORIAL.md">Tutorial</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/loganbnielsen/sol/releases">Releases</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/loganbnielsen/sol/blob/main/docs/planning/ROADMAP.md">Roadmap</a>
        </nav>
        <span>© 2026 Sol Fabricator · Apache-2.0</span>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </main>
  <script>
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-copy]').forEach((button) => {
      button.addEventListener('click', async () => {
        await navigator.clipboard.writeText(button.dataset.copy ?? '');
        button.textContent = 'Copied';
        window.setTimeout(() => button.textContent = 'Copy', 1400);
      });
    });
  </script>
</Base>
